{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact 7eb515280dc341672579f17d26ae7c141048f70d, under design request git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a2cbd459e96c2ddb55c84f34eddb76ea63d0cfca (#10286), answering review git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:197f8511c73eae628bd8e7333b793cce102ffb62 (#10554).\n\nRevision 2 materially improves the design. The direct-act binding, explicit thread restrictions and reserved overlap rule address the original examples. The immutable legacy vocabulary, recovery transition, operator correction, supersession inventory and removal of the unchecked key field are useful corrections. The replay witness table is substantially clearer. Six remaining design findings prevent approval.\n\n1. P1 — Bind the grantor's signed grant, not just the admitted delegation.\nnotes/2026-10-02-declared-acts.md@7eb515280dc341672579f17d26ae7c141048f70d:456 says a delegate op records the binding in force when admitted; roster remains a platform kind accepting v:1. The signed delegate body currently contains kinds or *, lanes and expiry, but no expected bindings (packages/contract/src/roster.ts@df22d771:99). A grantor signs a delegation for stage-change while it means [version]; before admission, policy changes it to [version, land]. The room now pins the new binding. The grantee can sign the new landing-capable act and passes the proposed delegation check, although the grantor never signed for that meaning. Expanding * only at admission similarly adds kinds introduced while the grant was in flight. The new pin protects grants already admitted, not signed grants in flight. Specify signed expected binding identities (and a signed wildcard expansion/catalog boundary), compare them at admission, and cover room-custody invitation/session grants as well. Add delayed explicit-kind and wildcard grant cases across activation, plus exact retries and legacy-to-v2 delegation treatment. Do not silently upgrade old unbound grants.\n\n2. P2 — Give step-semantics upgrades an ordered, retained identity.\nThe same file:425-433 hashes a platform steps version and says a platform release changing a step makes every binding stale. Sections 3.3, 4.1 and 5 instead select semantics solely from the last retained policy activation. The policy shape does not pin the steps version, and the note does not retain/select old step implementations as it explicitly does for evaluator profiles. After a code release changes take semantics, the same retained policy can therefore imply two bindings and two folds with no log boundary distinguishing them. Specify where the active steps version is durably pinned, how an authorized change takes effect at an exact sequence, and how admission and a future fresh-clone verifier choose retained semantics for each interval. Shipping a new engine must not silently reinterpret the room's existing document. Add a log spanning two steps versions, old accepted retries, and deployment without activation. This is separate from the evaluator-version proposal.\n\n3. P2 — Derive required evaluation calls and their inputs, including omissions.\nThe same file:607-611 leaves refuse/land replay unchanged and derives obligations from recorded decisions and contexts. Existing replayDecisions in packages/log/src/verify.ts@df22d771:421-457 groups only decisions present in the receipt; an empty set invokes nothing. A validly room-signed log can omit the entire matching require evaluation and its context, record an empty obligation set and later land. Merely replaying each present context and deriving effects from its outcomes does not detect that omission. A supplied context can also describe a different actor, lane or proposal unless checked against the independently derived fold/witness. Specify the complete required call sequence per step, reconstruct its deterministic inputs and budget progression, compare recorded calls/contexts/outcomes for both missing and extra material, then derive obligations/effects. Add forged-log fixtures deleting a whole require/refuse/land call and substituting a plausible but false context. Also distinguish the evidence that an integration was actually prepared for a specific operation from a tuple of integration/owner/tree reported only by the check witness; matching Git trees alone does not prove that preparation relationship. State any remaining limit explicitly. The current six fixtures do not close these gaps.\n\n4. P2 — Preserve scope changes during code-review takeover.\nThe same file:170 makes scope a rescope-only field and :122-125 says a thread's scope never changes, while section 6 promises the existing code-review behavior with only bindings added. Current reclaim reads body.scope, checks its overlaps and updates it for both rescope and takeover (packages/room/src/admission.ts@df22d771:662-710); even existing takeover tests send scope (packages/room/test/workerd/lanes.test.ts@df22d771:49-65). A released body-scoped claim can currently be taken over with a new scope. The proposed take loses that behavior or rejects its old body. Separate the immutable scope source/fixed template from a body-scoped thread's current scope. Define takeover with body.scope for the code-review declaration, preserving overlap, generation and fencing rules, while keeping solo/part fixed scopes immutable. Include a released claim taken over with a different scope and an overlapping-scope refusal. Also make the v2 suite criterion honest about the intentional recovery-op translation: bindings alone cannot transform purpose:config-recovery into recover.\n\n5. P2 — Complete the jam's check path and define how jobs choose an act.\nThe same file:978-1069 gives the jam's acts, but none has a check step. Sections 7.2 and 7.3 require an in-key check before a pattern landing publishes; sections 3.6 and 8.1 say CheckJob gains a kind/binding but do not define selection when zero or multiple declarations can submit that evidence. With this example there is no admissible result kind: literal check is undeclared and no other act can meet the obligation. Add the jam's check declaration, its thread applicability and checker role, and specify an unambiguous job-to-act mapping with validation for missing/ambiguous kinds and required extra body fields. This is admission/design work distinct from the existing open dispatch-provider finding. Add an end-to-end pattern -\u003e prepared integration -\u003e signed declared check -\u003e published landing acceptance case, and missing/ambiguous mapping failures.\n\n6. P2 — Reconcile retirement with thread-reference validation.\nThe same file:371 requires every threads name to be a currently declared opening kind (or room), but :723 says a hold opened by a retired kind can end through a declared act whose threads names that retired kind. Those rules cannot both hold. Retiring take-part while preserving leave for existing part threads makes the replacement policy invalid; removing take-part from leave prevents those holds from being released through it. Define how historical opening kinds remain referencable without remaining available to open new threads, including how kind-name reuse is distinguished or intentionally handled. Add retirement with an open hold/version and a remaining release/review path, and the corresponding unknown-name validation case.\n\nArchitecture: In gitseq's layer terminology, this is proposed Workroom/interpreter and projection behavior (layers 5-7), with Room host execution boundaries (4) and Git-backed evidence/landing (1); it changes no running kernel/nexus behavior (2-3). Closed primitive semantics plus data declarations, one policy activation boundary and retained historical interpretation remain the right division. The note is documentation only and expressly commissions no build; the protocol/type amendment remains a separate reviewed stage. Findings 2, 3 and 6 are necessary to make its history/activation contract coherent. No new platform implementation or deployment is authorized by this review.\n\nSecurity: Direct signatures, current authority checks, admin approval of configuration, recovery outside declarations, bounded shapes and conservative overlap are preserved in intent. Findings 1 and 3 leave an authority race and an overstated independent-proof guarantee. I re-inspected effective operator direction git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4e4134b406fda6d8764b5d215c86db4ae5f160fa (#10285), correction git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b2cdc44a4fd2967f4ecd6a366dfa67df36f513b8 (#10289), the documentation-only commission, checker promise and both exact-head artifacts. Builder now correctly owns the self-hostability judgment; the separate gate-1 recommendation stays a proposal for Hugh. This review does not adopt the marked product decisions or extend live authority.\n\nSimplification: Keep the single policy document, finite steps and per-kind bindings. Resolve shared rules once (scope source versus current scope, grant binding, job selection and historical kind references) and have both worked examples use them. Extra duplicated state or a second configuration channel is unnecessary. Keep the full functionality; estimates and the October 5 recommendation may sequence it, not delete it. The R-LANE-2 discrepancy is now explicitly retained as a separate implementation gap.\n\nEvidence and limits: Read the complete 1,452-line revision 2 note and README delta in a clean detached clone at the exact head; inspected all two current artifacts and direct promise provenance; both implementing commits carry the full request trailer. git diff --check passed. Relative to df22d771 the head changes only this note and two README lines; packages, tests, manifests and lockfile are unchanged. Cross-checked the cited current takeover, signed delegation, replay-context and replay-decisions source plus existing lane tests. These are concrete design counterexamples, not claims of executing an unimplemented v2 runtime. No new runtime guard exists in this head to mutate. I did not rerun root gates solely for this documentation revision; the artifact's reported test/typecheck results remain the builder's evidence. The prior-art reads from revision 1 are carried, not represented as new research.\n\nPlease revise on the same implementation request/promise and republish both artifacts at one exact head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b2453f5b97b410ade5267fceb6559aaddc01e550","binding":"assigned","head":"7eb515280dc341672579f17d26ae7c141048f70d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5e099898f532f1342d64fd89d248dbd584907793\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e635e71eca17ba273ad50729fd39b4380e887485\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ccce390e586bed6d86d107fb20f357c57d92f0f7","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}